WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.659 --> 00:00:04.930
I have completely unsurprising.

00:00:04.930 --> 00:00:12.990
Shocking news for you today, a 50 year study
of tax cuts has determined that when you give

00:00:12.990 --> 00:00:20.099
the very rich tax cuts, things don't trickle
down and inequality actually gets worse.

00:00:20.099 --> 00:00:25.769
Now, if you understand economics at all, you
would say, David, how is this even a story?

00:00:25.769 --> 00:00:29.760
But there's a large part of this country that
still thinks you give tax cuts to the rich,

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:33.430
money trickles down and it reduces inequality
and everything's good.

00:00:33.430 --> 00:00:39.149
This is a paper from the London School of
Economics and King's College, London.

00:00:39.149 --> 00:00:44.110
And it looked at 50 years of tax cuts for
the wealthy across 18 different countries.

00:00:44.110 --> 00:00:49.750
And they evaluated claims like trickle down
tax cuts for the rich, trickle down and help

00:00:49.750 --> 00:00:54.150
the economy or cutting taxes on the rich reduces
inequality.

00:00:54.150 --> 00:01:00.360
And what they found is that cutting taxes
on the rich increases inequality, gives the

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:06.780
rich more money with almost nothing trickling
down below overall, increases inequality and

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:09.240
doesn't improve economic performance.

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:12.190
Overall, it doesn't lead to economic growth.

00:01:12.190 --> 00:01:14.460
It doesn't lead to employment growth.

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:16.980
It just causes higher income inequality.

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:24.810
And one of the things that is important to
understand about this is that there are always

00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:28.850
going to be let's divide this up into two
ways.

00:01:28.850 --> 00:01:35.120
I think that for a lot of just average people
who don't study economics, this idea was always

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:40.750
a hard sell, I think, to just normal folks
who have not been radicalized to fall for

00:01:40.750 --> 00:01:41.850
trickle down economics.

00:01:41.850 --> 00:01:45.492
If you go to normal people who just work a
job and don't think too much about it, and

00:01:45.492 --> 00:01:50.250
you say, hey, if we give tax cuts to the really
rich, do you think it helps you?

00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:56.710
You go to a Laundromat owner or a hardware
store owner or a teacher and you say, hey,

00:01:56.710 --> 00:02:01.220
we're giving billionaires and people with
over 50 million in assets, we're giving them

00:02:01.220 --> 00:02:02.220
a tax cut.

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:03.530
Do you think this will help you?

00:02:03.530 --> 00:02:06.580
I think that's always been a hard sell for
the average person.

00:02:06.580 --> 00:02:13.620
But there is an entire movement that is based
around paying people at think tanks and other

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:18.920
places to claim that tax cuts for the rich
are going to help everybody in some way.

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:25.340
And the assumption that really fails is that
when you cut taxes for the rich, more people

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:28.860
are hired and it fails for a number of different
reasons.

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:33.410
First of all, most of the money doesn't even
enter the ecosystem of where it would affect

00:02:33.410 --> 00:02:37.650
hiring because rich people have a much higher
savings rate than poor people.

00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:43.850
If you give a poor person a five percent reduction
in their tax taxes, they're almost certainly

00:02:43.850 --> 00:02:45.130
going to spend all of that money.

00:02:45.130 --> 00:02:49.331
If you give a really, really rich person a
five percent reduction in their taxes, they're

00:02:49.331 --> 00:02:53.190
going to save most of that money because they're
not counting on that money to live.

00:02:53.190 --> 00:02:57.570
And as a result, because the money is just
being saved, it's not being spent by rich

00:02:57.570 --> 00:03:02.680
people in a way that creates a need for more
employees or a need to pay employees more

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:03.690
money.

00:03:03.690 --> 00:03:10.950
Number two, even the money that the rich do
spend out of business, only part of that goes

00:03:10.950 --> 00:03:18.450
to wages and mostly that additional spending
that the rich do goes to profits.

00:03:18.450 --> 00:03:21.910
And part of the hard costs of the things that
they are buying.

00:03:21.910 --> 00:03:24.910
The driver of hiring, we know, is demand.

00:03:24.910 --> 00:03:26.650
It's not labor supply.

00:03:26.650 --> 00:03:30.210
And this is often an argument that's missing
when talking about illegal immigration or

00:03:30.210 --> 00:03:36.510
immigration in general, the argument that
we hear goes they come here legally or illegally.

00:03:36.510 --> 00:03:39.000
Doesn't our economic argument, it doesn't
matter.

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:46.830
They take our jobs and because they increase
the supply of labor, wages go down.

00:03:46.830 --> 00:03:48.110
It's basic economics.

00:03:48.110 --> 00:03:53.400
They say you bring in a lot more people looking
for work and companies can pay less and they're

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:55.860
stealing jobs and lowering wages.

00:03:55.860 --> 00:04:00.790
Well, remember, wages are only part of the
cost of a good or service.

00:04:00.790 --> 00:04:07.709
And remember that all of those immigrants
in your community, legal or illegal immigrants,

00:04:07.709 --> 00:04:08.709
they also buy stuff.

00:04:08.709 --> 00:04:11.990
They also rent apartments, they also buy food,
they go to restaurants.

00:04:11.990 --> 00:04:18.380
So what's missing from this overly simplistic
analysis is that when you have immigrants

00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:24.980
come in looking for work, it's a huge increase
to demand because you have more people demanding

00:04:24.980 --> 00:04:31.240
products and services, which under the same
simple economic argument would mean businesses

00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:33.260
can raise prices and make more money.

00:04:33.260 --> 00:04:34.260
Right.

00:04:34.260 --> 00:04:40.680
And people only talk about supply of labor
to argue against immigration and ignore the

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:44.900
other side of that, which is the demand increase,
which is good for businesses.

00:04:44.900 --> 00:04:49.080
And this is all been demonstrated academically
by countless economic papers.

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:53.280
So what's missing you anybody surprised to
get back to the main article?

00:04:53.280 --> 00:05:00.260
Anybody surprised that over 50 years in 18
different countries, cutting taxes for the

00:05:00.260 --> 00:05:04.530
rich didn't trickle down, but it just increased
income inequality.

00:05:04.530 --> 00:05:10.020
You lack and I'm not saying this pejoratively,
it sounds like you just lack a more holistic

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:11.990
macroeconomic understanding.

00:05:11.990 --> 00:05:16.550
Of course, cutting taxes for the rich didn't
stimulate the economy in the way that some

00:05:16.550 --> 00:05:18.470
of these supply siders think.

00:05:18.470 --> 00:05:21.509
Let me know your thoughts, but a completely
unsurprising result.

